[
 {:seon.task/name "a-context-is-ordered-by-when-each-item-was-written"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-11: \"I want all IDs to be unique among all the branches and for us to use time for ordering not TX-ids\" (docs/decisions.md). This replaces the sort-key plan of 2026-11-08 (one stored :seon.ctx/sort-key a process wrote): nothing is stored; a pattern asks the transaction that wrote each member's link ({[:ns/_attr :tx :at] ..}, docs/ctx.md#the-pattern) and a reader orders by its time, ties (two branches in one millisecond) by the store-wide transaction id.

Relanded on jolt/plantedhalf (Web, reviewed by Context and Data model, 2026-11-11), landing after the store's erase: msgorder (its revert reverted) on jolt/rolesource (a stored pattern names its role, so its :tx :at are the schema's) and jolt/taskreasons (a required join asked without its :tx is refused, seon.ctx/lost-required); the window fix was already on main (f7eea19ab1, proved by seon.ctx-test/a-windowed-join-asked-its-transaction-patches-as-a-build); a law that every history join a schema's pattern declares asks :tx (seon.ctx-test/a-pattern-joins-refs-by-their-attribute). seon.ctx/placed-messages reads no db and places, from the pull, every join whose target is history (the entity index's :history-schemas, read from the join's attribute): the joins whose members carry a turn number make the turns, the others are placed by their link's :at, a member that refs a turn's call stays under it; page and request read that one placement by path. Proofs: seon.ctx-test/an-own-pattern-written-before-a-join-asked-its-transaction-still-places-its-messages, seon.render-test/a-planted-entity-reaches-the-page and -request, a-join-whose-target-is-no-history-is-no-conversation; recorded requests unchanged by a byte but the :example session's :at (its own commit). A store not erased keeps the batch-47 hole (a whole copy stored without :tx places nothing): the erase is part of this landing.

Left, each a reader that still orders by entity id, moved to (comp :db/txInstant :at): seon.ctx/turn-evals (a turn's evals by eid); seon.repl/again (the eval before, by eid); seon.render.ui's agent render sorting :evals by key; seon.root taking the newest agent of a key by :db/id; the :limit :reverse windows in the root fact's pattern and the callers join (newest n by eid).

Proof to write, red first: seon.ctx-test/a-contexts-items-sort-by-when-they-were-written (a reply's evals and a mid-turn message in time order with their ids scrambled); recorded_requests unchanged."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/a-contexts-items-sort-by-when-they-were-written}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-turn-shows-how-main-diverged"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: :will-merge (seon.merge/divergence against the agent's merge base) shows what the branch carries; nothing shows what main changed since that base, so a coming conflict is met only at the merge. Stage 5 of docs/users.md#the-stages-agreed-2026-11-09 (seon.merge/a-session-syncs-with-main-unless-private) takes main in at each turn for a syncing session and shows what holds a change as unmerged entries on the page parts; this task is what a private session, which syncs only when saved, reads instead.

The change: one producer of main's changes since the base intersected with the branch's, rendered each turn as {:main-ahead n :conflict [..] :you-call [..] :other n}: names changed by both, names changed on main that the branch's changes call (:form/refs), the rest counted. Its cost follows the changes since the base, not the program, by the index diff (seon.merge/delta-changes, docs/merging.md).

Waits on stage 5, which decides whether a private session keeps a base of its own."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/a-turn-names-mains-coming-conflict}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-first-turns-context-costs-what-it-shows"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the parts of a request have cost proofs of their own in `seon.ctx.wire-cost-test` (the status, the task's plan with its cites, the search's push, a request's records), but a first request whole, by part, in tokens is measured only by hand. Measured on main 850fbffbcc (seon_context, task seon.render/a-model-render-draws-each-turns-diff): about 23.4k tokens, of which the system message 8.5k characters, the task plan 20.4k and the task's cites 51.3k for 45 names in plan order.

The change: a cost proof on a production-shaped world for a first request's parts (the cites, the search, the status) in ticks, bytes and tokens, each part costing the text it shows. The size of what is shown is the area primer's plan (Context, 2026-11-10), not this task.

Proofs: a first request's cost proof by part (seon.cost/proof)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/production-shaped-world-first-request-s-cites-search}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-status-declares-its-summary"
  :seon.task/plan "What was seen, in the lab, 2026-11-04: 22 stray red tests made the status 32k tokens a turn, repeated on the page each request. A prototype render, each red's name, its first failure and a form that reads it, made it 2k. What should be true: the status's declared model render is that summary, any agent can override it, and the whole is a read away, by a form the render names. The no-cuts ruling of 2026-10-30 is about an agent's own values: this is a render's choice with the rest a form away, and the owner's call. my.agent/red does not exist yet."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/many-red-tests-draw-a-summary-with-what-reads-the-rest}}
 {:seon.task/name "teaching-is-data"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-05: the agent's entity \"teaches them about the system, it explain what task they are working on, how messaging works when they get a message, evertyhing. It's all data driven\"; \"NO SPECIAL CASE FOR CITING\"; \"NO OTHER MECHANISMS\".

What exists outside data: the system message is docs/prompt.md's text (seon.ctx.wire/system-text; on stage 2b's branch, sections named by :seon.agent/system); seon.model/eval-tool's descriptions teach how to evaluate; seon.ctx.session's session-open and the example session tags frame a session in fixed words; citing has writers of its own (seon.render.ui/page-cites, seon.task/agent-cites-tx, :seon.config.agent/cites, my.agent/cite! and uncite!); seon.ctx.wire/help-text and seon.learn are text beside the entity.

The change: what an agent reads is data on its entity, or on entities it refs, rendered by their schemas' renders: the prompt's sections by ref, how messaging works by the message's render, how a task is worked by the task's. eval-tool's descriptions say only the tool's shape. Citing is ordinary data and swap!. What an agent adds of its own is a key and a render (docs/ctx.md#adding-a-datum-and-its-render).

Deletes: session-open and the example tags' fixed words where a render can say them; page-cites, agent-cites-tx, :seon.config.agent/cites, my.agent/cite! and uncite!; help-text's copy of what the wiki holds.

Proofs: two agents with different data read different context with no code changed; the system message's words come from the entity's refs. Judged by real agents' turns to green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/two-agents-different-data-their-entities-read-different}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-models-readers-follow-the-re-models"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: item 1 waits on Optimization's re-model 1 (run measures as entities, jolt/sync), item 3 needs a schema-property design, and item 2 has landed; Optimization lands re-model 1, then Context splits what is left into a task with a proof. Context's readers that move when Optimization's re-models land (docs/users.md#the-stages-agreed-2026-11-09), each in the same branch as its schema change or right after it:
1. A run's measures are entities of their own, a ref to what they measured, summed when read (re-model 1, Optimization, on jolt/sync): seon.ctx.status's readers of :seon.test/ticks, :seon.test/bytes and :seon.fn/stats (status, task-of's :proved, the var pattern's :seon.test/* attributes) read the measures by their ref.
2. Landed (jolt/cite-ref, 2026-11-09): a cite's target is a ref to an entity that exists, and `seon.ctx-test/a-pattern-joins-refs-by-their-attribute` holds every pattern's joins keyed by their attribute.
3. seon.ctx's write! refuses an edit of a var through swap! by checking :var/name in code; instead a property on the var's and form's entity schemas says the entity is changed by its definition, and the edit walk reads it."
  :seon.task/proofs #{}
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-proofs-example-is-the-nearest-test-of-what-it-changes"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the status shows an unwritten proof its namespace's shortest test that uses a helper (seon.ctx.status/test-shape), whatever the task changes. In the T3 rerun (seon.render/the-root-tile-counts-the-program-it-loaded, 2026-11-10) the example drew whole page components (eval-html) where the task needed one tile rendered with a plain ctx ({:seon.ctx/ctx {:process value}}), which the agent found by trying.

The change: the example is the shortest test that calls a function the task cites (seon.program's callers of each cited var, tests only), else today's choice; one lookup per cited var.

Proof: an unwritten proof of a task citing seon.render.ui/process-tile shows as its example a test that calls process-tile."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/unwritten-proof-task-citing-seon-render-ui-process}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-edit-says-when-it-repaired-the-forms"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: seon.repl/edit! reads the new text with the forgiving reader (seon.reader/forgive), which inserts or drops delimiters, and says so only in the answer's :repaired key. In the T3 rerun the agent spent an eval re-reading its edit to see what the repair did.

The change: docs/prompt.md's line on edit! says it repairs delimiters and that :repaired names each repair (seon.ctx.search/repaired's shape), and the eval's printed answer leads with the repair when there is one.

Proof: an edit! whose text drops a closing paren answers with its repair named first in the printed text."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/edit-text-drops-closing-paren-answers-repair-named}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-system-prompt-holds-what-agents-use"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: docs/prompt.md is about 8.6k chars, every agent's first message. Three baseline agents and the T3 rerun (2026-11-10) each named most of it unused: branches and merging, the page's CSS and its edit format; what they used was the tool list, proofs, and the REPL's rules.

The change, by measurement: for each section of docs/prompt.md, count over a set of real runs (the curated tasks) the evals that use what it says (a form it names, a rule it states, a failure it explains) and the tokens it costs per request; a section no run used moves to the wiki page it belongs to, cited from the prompt by its address, so an agent reads it on demand. The prompt keeps what a first turn needs.

Proof: a cost proof of the system message's size, and the curated tasks' turns to green no worse after the cut."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/system-message-holds-docs-prompt-md-section-curated}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-components-change-sends-what-moved-inside-it"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a turn's page message holds editscript edits between the page parts at two bases (`seon.ctx.wire/page-diff-msgs`, the parts drawn by `seon.ctx.wire/page-parts-through` and compared by `seon.render/page-edits`; since 2026-11-06 a change is editscript edits by name, `seon.render/component-edits`, which resends a whole part only when that is shorter: docs/render.md#a-change-is-edits-by-name). In the T3 rerun (on main's page evals, before stage 2b) the agent's own edits to seon.render.ui/process-tile resent the tile whole three times, about 3k chars each.

What is left: measure on 2b's page message, for a cited function edited at the REPL, the bytes its turn's page message carries against the bytes that changed in its source (no test states it; seon.render-test/a-change-speaks-in-names covers the edits' shape); where the whole part is still resent for a small change, find why `component-edits` chose it, the owner's rule (2026-11-10): what changed is shown in full, a removal is one line.

Proof: a cited var's one-line edit gives a page message under the size of the var's source."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/line-edit-cited-var-gives-turn-s-page}}
 {:seon.task/name "ctx-reads-attribute-owners-by-the-one-lookup"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-10: \"No duplicate implementations\". Which entity schema owns an attribute is read through one function, seon.db/owners (the write check, seon.db/most-specific, seon.render/schema-of and entity-schema call it; Web, jolt/walk-2a). Three readers in seon.ctx still read the entity index's :entity-schemas inline: seon.ctx's time-ordered join test (a reverse join's members by store-issued id) and the patch's new-member identity check and its refusal text. Moved to owners as one call each, seon.ctx-test/a-lookups-patch-costs-its-change went from 31 to 33 contracted calls at n=20 (its bound 31, allowed 1.0x), since owners is contracted and these run a member at a time. The change: read the owners of a patch's attributes once per patch (one call), and of a join's attribute once where the join is built, so the cost proof stays at its bound. Proof: that cost proof unchanged; no (:entity-schemas ix) left in src outside seon.db/owners."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/entity-schemas-ix-left-src-outside-seon-db}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-context-shows-how-a-wiki-section-is-edited"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: in curated run 1 (2026-11-10) an agent spent four evals learning to edit a wiki section: (seon.repl/edit! \"docs/clocks.md\" ...) failed (\"no file at /private/tmp/seon-agents/docs/clocks.md: write it whole first\"), a search, a doc read, then a whitespace failure; the agent that read (doc seon.repl/edit!) first got the section-address form at once.

The change: the system prompt's edit! line shows the three forms, a var, a file path and a wiki section's address (\"docs/x.md#anchor\"), and the file-path refusal names the section form when the path is a wiki page.

Proof: edit! of a wiki page by path answers an error naming the section-address form."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/edit-wiki-page-file-path-answers-error-naming}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-stale-section-block-says-how-to-clear-it"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a change to a var's contract or docstring stales the wiki sections that cite it, and the status shows them under :attention, a read-and-probably-fix that does not refuse done (seon.problems/stale-sections, seon.problems/caused-split; docs/health.md#a-stale-section). In curated pair 2 (2026-11-10) both agents were sent to docs/resolution.md#sanctioned and docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach; one edited the sections, the other found my.doc/reviewed! by trying, and then hit its arity (\"it is called as (my.doc/reviewed! address who)\").

The change: the status's attention line for a stale section (its text says only: read it, then edit the section or review it) names, per section, its address, the var that changed, and the two forms that clear it, ready to evaluate: (seon.repl/edit! \"docs/x.md#y\" old new) and (my.doc/reviewed! \"docs/x.md#y\" \"<agent key>\").

Proof: an agent whose change stales a section reads, in its status, each section with both forms; evaluating the second clears the attention."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/agent-change-stales-section-reads-status-section-s}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-first-namespace-from-the-prompt-works-the-first-time"
  :seon.task/plan "A demo work agent (roles demo, 2026-11-10, agent h02459d3b542ab77) spent three evals before its first definition held, each on something its context should have made plain: (a) its namespace eval threw 'class clojure.lang.PersistentList cannot be cast to class clojure.lang.Symbol' although the ns form itself matched docs/prompt.md's line (it was sent together with a quoted (eval '(defn ...)) after it, and a variant with a escaped newline inside the string): find which form threw and whether the error names it; an error that does not say which form of a multi-form eval failed is the cause to fix, in the eval's error, not in the prompt; (b) a test called demo.math/double, which it never defined (No such var): the error is right; nothing to change unless the context showed a name it lacked; (c) its contract used :number, which malli does not have (number? does): the contract error should name the unknown schema and what malli offers (number?, :int, :double), and docs/prompt.md's contract line should show a non-int example. Reproduce each at n=1 in a worktree root first; record what the agent saw (seon_context) beside each fix."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-multi-form-eval-names-the-form-that-threw seon.contract-test/an-unknown-schema-in-a-contract-names-it}
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "ctx-holds-what-is-the-agents-and-names-the-rest"
  :seon.task/plan "Rule: an agent's pull holds its own attributes, what points at it by a reverse ref and its task; of anything else it holds the identity, and a render reads that thing's content from the db by a pattern its schema declares (docs/ctx.md#the-pattern). Measured once, on the demo agent, 2026-11-11: `@ctx` printed is 143,742 characters, 92.8% of it the content of its cites; a small agent's is 13,153. One agent, not the system's number. The cite's move is built on branch jolt/citepull (five cites 12,717 to 1,582 characters) and is nearly all of it: about 11,100 characters after. What is left, measured on main's 440 tasks: a proof's var is 3,076 characters on average, 63% the test's source and 25% the names it refers to (78 tasks have a proof on main; 9 in 10 hold under 2,686, the worst 17,094); 16 tasks have needs, the worst 6,533; no task holds an example session. Not to be built until a measure asks for it: the status reads each proof's verdict, failures and source from the pull at every turn, so moving the source out means the busiest render reads the db at every draw, to save about 3,000 characters a proof in a value an agent no longer reads whole at its first point. Worth it only if runs show agents printing their proofs or their task whole. If built, one small tip: a proof in the pull is its name, its verdict and its failures, and the status render reads the names it refers to from the db; a need is its id and what the done rule reads of it; the two pattern-only keys the cite's render reads on branch jolt/citepull (the target's and the owner's read patterns) become one NEW property on the cite's own schema, which does not exist yet and whose name is to be settled at a REPL (not reads under seon.render: another plan uses that name for something else); the task's search query, a copy of the plan, is read from the plan. Example sessions and started agents stay as they are until a task holds one. Proof, red first: a cost proof that `@ctx` printed does not grow with the size of what the agent cites, proves or needs (n = their content's characters, expected constant), its bound the printer's own; and each moved render shows what it showed, read from the db."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/ctx-does-not-grow-with-what-it-names}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-pull-holds-nothing-its-reader-cannot-read"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11 reading a first request as an agent. Three things in `@ctx` said nothing to their reader: the join of the system, printed as a map of one id to a map of that id; each cite's owner, a map holding only the agent's own id, under every cite the agent owns; and the task's kind, :whole, where its file says :work. Rule (docs/ctx.md#the-pattern): a join the pattern asks holds something its reader can use, an identity attribute at least, or the pattern does not ask it. Plan: the agent's pattern asks its system by an identity it can read or not at all (its reader is the page's link, which needs the system's id); a cite an agent owns does not join its owner (the agent is the root of its own pull; a task's cite keeps the task's id, which says whose it is); the kind an attach writes is read first (seon.task's write at an attach), and whether :whole is a second word for what the file calls :work. Proof, red first: over a seeded agent's pull, no join's member is a map of :db/id alone."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/no-join-holds-a-bare-id}}
 {:seon.task/name "grep-says-what-it-searched"
  :seon.task/plan "Loop R row R2 (2026-11-11, t11-t19: 'Maybe grep searches program forms only, not strings? ... grep gives nothing even for (doc \"') and Data model's real task ((grep \"text\") answered the agent's own task plan among its hits, and a plan that names a keyword reads as code). What exists: my.agent/grep's docstring lists the kinds (:var :test :section :task :schema) and that a string is matched through the search index by its words then as written; nothing in its ANSWER says what was searched, and :task is in by default. The change, as every tool's answer (seon.render/answer, docs/render.md#a-render-is-a-value-printed-once): grep's answer carries its schema (:my.agent/grep), whose description says what it searched and :seon.render/more the forms that narrow or widen it (:in, :ns, a regex), (the asker's own task leaving the hits is my.agent/grep-leaves-out-the-askers-own-task). Proof: grep's printed answer says what kinds it searched and the form that widens or narrows it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/grep-says-what-it-searched}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-require-at-the-repl-says-the-form-that-records-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Data model's real task, 2026-11-11 (finding 10): a (require ...) at the REPL is warned 'not program ... goes in its namespace's ns form' with no form that does it. The warning names the ns form to edit, as an edit! form ready to evaluate (seon.repl/edit! of the namespace's ns form, the require added), as other findings carry their fix. Proof: a require at the REPL answers a :seon.repl/fix holding an edit! form that, evaluated, makes the namespace's ns form require it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-require-at-the-repl-answers-the-edit-that-records-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-example-keeps-any-history-entity-by-its-schema"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11 ruling on what an example session records. ```seon.ctx.session/session-tx``` re-keys each record of a session by a list kept by hand in its namespace: for each schema an example holds, its id attribute, its agent attribute and its model-call attribute. All three are in the schema already: the identity key, the ref whose target is an agent, the ref whose target is a model call (docs/data-modeling.md#things-relate-by-refs). So a new history entity (a planted note) is refused by an example until someone adds a line to the list. Plan: the three are read from the entity index for any history entity an agent's pattern joins, and the list goes; an entity with no identity key is still refused, naming its schema. Cost: one lookup a record. Proof, red first: a schema registered on a branch, joined in a pattern, is kept by an example of that agent with its ids re-keyed, with no edit to seon.ctx.session."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx-test/an-example-keeps-a-history-entity-its-schema-describes}}
]
